1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,720
OAuth2 delegates access to the harbor API.
It does not, by itself, define how a
client learns the user’s identity in a
standard way.

2
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:22,247
OpenID Connect sits on top of OAuth2 and
adds that identity layer — the difference
between “this calendar client may read
berth windows” and “this browser session
is Alice from the company IdP.” The short
version:

3
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:32,640
OIDC keeps the authorization code flow you
already know, and adds an ID token — a JWT
that asserts who authenticated — plus a
UserInfo endpoint for profile claims.

4
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:45,311
Scopes like openid, profile, and email are
the handshake that you want login, not
only API access. Request openid and a
conformant provider returns an ID token
alongside the access token.

5
00:00:45,431 --> 00:01:00,527
Why harbor teams care: “Sign in with
company Okta / Azure AD / Keycloak”
without inventing a proprietary profile
JSON for every IdP. OIDC standardizes sub,
iss, aud, exp, nonce, and optional profile
claims.

6
00:01:00,647 --> 00:01:15,263
Your app verifies the ID token,
establishes a local session or security
context, and optionally still uses the
access token to call harbor APIs. Look at
this java for a moment. Hold the token
types apart.

7
00:01:15,384 --> 00:01:30,120
The ID token is for the client application
to know who logged in. The access token is
for calling a resource server. Your SPA
should not send the ID token as an API
bearer credential just because it is also
a JWT.

8
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:45,191
Resource servers validate access tokens
(and audience/scope). Login apps validate
ID tokens. Nonce and state are not
decorative. State ties the callback to the
browser session that started the redirect.

9
00:01:45,311 --> 00:01:51,719
Nonce binds the ID token to that
authentication attempt so replayed ID
tokens are harder to abuse.

10
00:01:51,840 --> 00:02:01,224
Spring’s OAuth2 login support handles the
common case when you use the client
starter correctly — still know why those
parameters exist when something fails in
production.

11
00:02:01,344 --> 00:02:17,424
A topic-specific misconception is saying
“OIDC replaces OAuth2.” It extends it.
Another is assuming every JWT from the
company IdP is interchangeable: access
tokens and ID tokens have different
audiences and purposes.

12
00:02:17,544 --> 00:02:27,384
A third is mapping email to local admin
without an explicit claim-to-authority
policy — that is how contractors inherit
berth-admin god mode from a misconfigured
IdP group.

13
00:02:27,504 --> 00:02:39,024
In practice we added identity assertions
on top of delegation: openid scope, ID
token validation, OidcUser in the security
context, and a clear split between login
tokens and API tokens.

14
00:02:39,144 --> 00:02:52,560
HTTP and login rules still live at the
edge. Many domain rules — “only the owner
may release this cargo” — want to sit next
to the service method itself. That is
where method security earns its place.
